; fault_fetch.kasm -- jumping to an odd address faults at the transfer.
;
; The last of the execute-time faults, and the one that survived longest
; unpinned: with the check deleted from k2g_core.sv, every testbench and the
; whole cosimulation still passed.
;
; It is detected at the prime rather than at the transfer (spec 6.4): the
; `DISPA` is the instruction that computed the bad target, and the `TC` may be
; a long way from it -- §6.6's two-target idiom puts a whole predicated branch
; in between. So `fault_addr` names the `DISPA` and the `TC` is never reached.
;
; Nothing is committed on the way out either, which is the part the two
; machines have to agree on and the reason this program exists: the emulator
; used to consume the jump latch before validating the target, so a faulting
; transfer cleared architectural state the RTL kept. The cosimulation compares
; `nisa`, and caught it.
; expect: FAULT_MISALIGNED_FETCH

        .org    0

        puc32   r1, 7           ; odd, so not a legal instruction address
        dispa   r1              ; MISALIGNED_FETCH, right here
        tc                      ; never reached
        halt
